    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72832.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 11,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think that we'll find that a number of my house Democratic colleagues that will oppose the cuts regardless of what level they are. There will be some on the Republican side who will believe that they're not They're not big enough. And so we'll have both of those sides making those arguments. And I think what we have to convince members of the committee is that this is a work in progress and that we need to get a bill out of the committee and we need to get a bill out of the House floor. We get the conference and we'll have to make compromises in a conference committee bill. And I think that's what's going to occur at the end of the day. There are certain reforms, I think, that Congressman Peterson and I have looked at with regards to the SNAP program. But the nutrition programs, we must remember providing very important safety net. We've had difficult times in this recession over the last four years. And point of fact, when folks who don't have sufficient economic means to get a decent healthy diet each day. Those food stamps, the SNAP program, also consume a lot of agricultural products. So, you know, those are products that people are consuming. And so we should not lose sight of that fact either.",
