  {
    "episode_id": 72832,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72832.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 17,
    "turn_text": "It eliminates a number of the programs that were no longer effective, it creates an insurance an insurance program as a safety net. But that insurance program, I think, has to be balanced with trying to ensure that supply stays within the demand. And there has to be some sort of tool or mechanism to do that. Now, to address Speaker Boehner's concerns, whether there's some way that we modify or provide some changes, I think people are willing to listen to. But the fact is, is that dairy producers have been hard hit. And if those bust and boom cycles continue as they have in the last decade, we're going to have more instability within the dairy industry, within producers. And while processors may like a ready supply of inexpensive milk, The producers, dairy men and dairy women, need to be able to have their monthly, as we used to say on the farm, cream check, exceed their monthly feed bill. I mean, when their input costs exceed the amount that they're getting for their milk check each month, you can't stand business like that long. And I'm not talking about dairies that have gotten leverage. I'm just talking about sound. during men and women who have been in this business for generations so i think you know there's a way we can work through this and uh... we've expressed that speaker bainer many of his individually and i think he wants to produce a farm bill at the end of the day so i don't think that uh... I just kind of believe that there's a way we can work through this and as we were forming the dairy title in the bill.",
