  {
    "episode_id": 72832,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72832.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 19,
    "turn_text": "Congressman Peterson and I did vote count last year and we think that we could have gotten a bill out of the house by a bipartisan vote and while obviously a lot of members have turned over as a result of last November's election, we're doing our due diligence and I think if we produce a well thought out Farm Bill that provides reform in the program crops reductions in various conservation programs and changes in reforms in the nutrition programs. Well, the numbers that we've put in committee or that may be in the house will probably not be the final numbers when we go to conference with a Senate bill. uh... ultimately i think we can we can produce issue got to remember two thousand and eight the uh... farm bill was the only piece of legislation that followed regular order and was passed into the president uh... he vetoed the farm bill we came back over wrote his veto and uh... he subsequently beat the one more time and we passed it uh... uh... second time and it became all Traditionally, the Farm Bill is one of the most bipartisan things we do here in Congress. Now, clearly, there will be differences between the right and the left, and some dynamics have changed. But I think at the end of the day, when we explain the importance of a Farm Bill from research, to market access programs, to conservation programs, to funding that is provided for detection and eradication of infestations When we look at assistance for rural development in our rural communities throughout America, we can make a strong case on why we need to have a farm bill. And I'm not even mentioning the crop insurance, which is the bottom line safety net for many of the commodities that are growing throughout America. So there's a lot of need out there, and we're being responsible by taking various cuts as we must do in every level of government. And I will hope very much that as we vet through this markup, that we'll find more individual members of Congress that understand that we're trying to find a balanced approach for the reauthorization of the Farm Bill.",
